Maintaining the opentui package
==============================

Which version to package
------------------------

Not necessarily the newest one. OpenTUI's TypeScript and its libopentui.so
talk over a private FFI ABI with no versioning of its own, so the two halves
have to come from the same release, and every consumer in the distribution
has to be built against that same release as well.

Today the only consumer is opencode, which pins the version in the catalog
block of its top level package.json:

    "@opentui/core": "0.5.12",
    "@opentui/keymap": "0.5.12",
    "@opentui/solid": "0.5.12",

So this package tracks that pin, not the newest tag. Bumping ahead of
opencode produces a library nothing in the distribution can load. When
opencode moves, bump both together and rebuild opencode.

Upstream moved from github.com/sst/opentui to github.com/anomalyco/opentui.
The old URL still redirects, but Source0 uses the new one.


Bumping the version
-------------------

1. Fetch the release tarball next to the spec:

       curl -L -o opentui-<version>.tar.gz \
           https://github.com/anomalyco/opentui/archive/refs/tags/v<version>.tar.gz

2. Check that the Zig version upstream demands is still the one Factory
   ships. It is a hard equality check in
   packages/native/build.zig (the Zig sources moved out of
   packages/core/src/zig in 0.5.5):

        const SUPPORTED_ZIG_VERSIONS = [_]SupportedZigVersion{
            .{ .major = 0, .minor = 16, .patch = 0 },
        };

   and the spec mirrors it in the zig_version global. The check is not
   over-caution on upstream's part: Zig is not source compatible across
   releases. If Factory's zig has moved past what upstream supports, the
   package has to be rebased onto a newer OpenTUI, not relaxed to a range.

   The unversioned zig BuildRequires resolves through the versioned
   zig0.16 package's Provides: zig = 0.16.0; that indirection is
   deliberate, so a Factory zig roll only retargets this package once a
   zig0.16 successor exists.

3. Check the highlighter's grammar modules. packages/core/src/lib/tree-sitter/
   parsers-config.ts pins the grammar releases upstream downloaded the
   .wasm files from; the package replaces them with the tree-sitter-<lang>-
   wasm packages' modules (see "What gets installed"). A grammar upstream
   adds fails %install until its package exists and is listed; a moved pin
   means re-checking the highlights.scm/injections.scm next to the module
   against the packaged grammar version (load the module and the query with
   web-tree-sitter; a query naming a node the grammar lacks throws).

4. Re-derive the License tag. Everything below is compiled into
   libopentui.so, so every copyleft or permissive-but-not-MIT licence in
   the vendored trees belongs in the tag; see "Vendored dependencies".

5. Re-check the two %check workarounds at the bottom of this file. The
   aarch64 test skip (opentui-aarch64-skip-crashing-x11-tests.patch)
   goes away if the crash is fixed; the /tmp/.X11-unix mkdir stays as
   long as the bind test binds filesystem sockets.

6. Drop the old tarballs, osc addremove, build, submit.


Vendored dependencies
----------------------

Everything the native library links is vendored in-tree; the build fetches
nothing. Two mechanisms, both hash-pinned by upstream:

packages/native/src/vendor/zig-deps.tar.gz holds the Zig dependencies -
yoga (the flexbox layout engine, v3.2.1, whose C++ sources are compiled
straight into the library), uucode (Unicode character data, in a ghostty
and an opentui flavour) and ghostty (the embedded-terminal VT engine,
1.3.2-dev). %build unpacks it with upstream's own
packages/native/scripts/prepare-zig-deps.sh into the ignored
packages/native/zig-deps directory, which build.zig.zon references over
relative paths. The pins and SHA-256 hashes live in
packages/native/src/vendor/update-zig-deps.sh; regenerating the archive is
upstream's `bun run vendor:update:zig`, not a maintainer script, so there
is no opentui_zigdeps equivalent anymore - the 0.4.5 bundle and script are
retired.

Next to it, packages/native/src/vendor/ carries the image C libraries as
plain sources, compiled straight in: lcms2 (2.19.1, MIT), libwebp (1.6.0,
BSD-3-Clause, decoder subset listed in libwebp/FILES), stb_image and
stb_image_resize2 (2.18, MIT-or-public-domain), wuffs (wuffs-v0.3.c,
Apache-2.0) and miniaudio (0.11.22, Unlicense-or-MIT-0). Their updater is
`bun run vendor:update:images`, driven by vendor/update.sh pins.

None of this is unbundled: the tree-sitter grammar modules stay the one
exception (see below), everything else has no system counterpart to link
against.


What gets installed, and why it is shaped that way
--------------------------------------------------

OpenTUI is published to npm as a set of pure TypeScript packages plus one
prebuilt native package per platform, chosen through optionalDependencies:

    @opentui/core                 the TypeScript
    @opentui/core-linux-x64       libopentui.so for that platform
    @opentui/core-linux-arm64     ... and so on

packages/core/src/platform/runtime-assets.bun.ts imports the matching one by
name to find the library. So the native half is installed under exactly the
name that import expects:

    %{_libdir}/opentui/@opentui/core-linux-<arch>/libopentui.so

together with the small index.js / index.bun.js / package.json shims that
make the directory resolvable as an npm package. Upstream writes those in
packages/core/scripts/build.ts, which needs bun to run; the spec writes them
directly instead, because nothing else in that script applies here.

The TypeScript goes next to it:

    %{_libdir}/opentui/@opentui/core
    %{_libdir}/opentui/@opentui/keymap
    %{_libdir}/opentui/@opentui/solid

This layout serves both ways a consumer can find OpenTUI:

  - symlink or copy %{_libdir}/opentui/@opentui into a node_modules
    directory and ordinary resolution finds both the TypeScript and the
    native package;

  - or set OTUI_ASSET_ROOT=%{_libdir}/opentui and the library is found
    directly. OTUI_ASSET_ROOT is upstream's own supported way to relocate
    runtime assets (packages/core/src/platform/assets.ts); it is consulted
    before the optionalDependency import, so it wins.

The TypeScript is in %{_libdir} rather than %{_datadir}, and opentui-devel is
not noarch, because it sits in the same tree as the native package it has to
resolve alongside.

One part of that tree is not upstream's file: the syntax highlighter's
grammar modules, packages/core/src/lib/tree-sitter/assets/<lang>/
tree-sitter-<lang>.wasm (javascript, typescript, markdown, markdown_inline,
zig), which upstream commits as downloads from the grammars' releases. The
distribution builds every tree-sitter grammar to WebAssembly in a
tree-sitter-<lang>-wasm subpackage (%treesitter_wasm_* macros, clang, lld
and wasi-libc through the tree-sitter CLI's own wasi-sdk invocation), so
%install turns those five files into symlinks to /usr/share/tree-sitter/wasm
and opentui-devel requires the four packages. The loop walks the asset
directories: a grammar upstream adds fails the build until its package is
in BuildRequires and the devel Requires. The highlight queries next to the
modules stay upstream's; they were checked against the packaged grammar
versions (javascript 0.23.1 and markdown 0.5.3 where upstream pins 0.25.0
and 0.5.1) when this was introduced, and belong on the bump checklist.


The build does not need bun
---------------------------

Worth knowing, because it is not obvious from upstream's scripts. Upstream
builds the native library with `bun run build:native`, but all that does is
unpack the vendored Zig dependencies (prepare-zig-deps.sh, plain shell -
the spec calls it directly) and shell out to

    cd packages/native && zig build -Doptimize=ReleaseFast

and then copy the result into a generated npm package directory. The spec
does both of those directly, so opentui does not build-depend on bun and is
not blocked on bun reaching Factory.

`-Dlibrary-target=native` is passed deliberately. Upstream's default for a
native build is the first entry in SUPPORTED_TARGETS matching the host,
which for Linux is a glibc-2.17-pinned cross target - Zig would then compile
against its own bundled libc headers and link against synthesised stubs
rather than the system glibc. buildSingleTarget passes any unrecognised
target string straight to std.Target.Query.parse, so "native" is accepted
and gives an ordinary system build. The library lands in
packages/native/lib/native/ either way; only the triple behind it
differs.

The baseline-CPU patch (opentui-baseline-cpu.patch) belongs to that flag:
"native" leaves the CPU model at host detection, so the patch pins it to
.baseline and %install fails the build if an aarch64 library ever carries
FEAT_LRCPC instructions again. The old build-ID patch is retired - upstream
sets lib.build_id itself since 0.5.10.


%check workarounds
------------------

Two, both clipboard-related, both re-check on every bump:

- opentui-aarch64-skip-crashing-x11-tests.patch skips the "expired
  initial INCR" and "delayed expired INCR" unit tests on aarch64, where
  they abort the test runner in drivePendingResponse with a null call
  through the faked xcb_request_check. The crash reproduces outside the
  build sandbox with pristine sources and the same code is still
  upstream, so it is an upstream aarch64 bug, not packaging fallout;
  upstream CI runs x86_64, where the suite passes. Drop the patch (or
  narrow it) once the crash is gone.
- %check creates /tmp/.X11-unix before running the suite: the "bare
  DISPLAY falls back to a filesystem listener" test binds a socket at
  /tmp/.X11-unix/X<display>, which exists on any X machine but not in
  the build root, and fails with ENOENT without it.
